WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.080
Thank you for having us.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.060
Can you start by introducing yourselves?

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.460
How did you form,
and where did your band name come from?

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.060
Who wants to start?

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.430
I'm Moeka Shiotsuka,
and I play guitar and vocals.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.740
I'm Yurika Kasai, and I play bass.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.510
I'm Hiroa Fukuda, and I play drums.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.350
We are Hitsujibungaku.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.860
How did we form?

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.850
I was invited to join the band.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.420
There were five of us at first.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:02.620
It was during high school,
when I was around 15 or 16.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.300
But everyone quit due to their future plans.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:15.010
Fukuda joined first,
and Yurika joined in 2016.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.970
We hadn't met before they joined.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.140
But I reached out to him after
seeing him play drums for another band

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:30.290
and searched for additional members,
and that's how this group formed.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:31.860
It's been about six years.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.390
We've been playing together
for about six years.
It's been about six years.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.130
It's been about six years.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:40.860
The band name is something I came up
with randomly in middle school.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.800
Oh, questions come from over here.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:56.710
If you were to come up with a new band name
using two random words, what would it be?

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.330
Since we're hitsuji and bungaku?
(Hitsuji = sheep, bungaku = literature)

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.680
I think so.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.630
I'd want to use English words.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.810
I don't like kanji.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.390
Sheep?

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.170
I prefer hitsuji.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.420
Tsuchi and— No, that's not it.
(Tsuchi = earth)

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.450
Daichi and kaze?
(Daichi = land, kaze = wind)

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.790
Daichi and kaze? That sounds so spiritual.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:37.050
Our element is earth,

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.770
You mean the shrine thing?

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.620
...and yours is wind.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.720
She's talking about shrine fortunes.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.360
So our band name would be
"Tsuchi to kaze." ("Earth and Wind")

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.060
Foreigners wouldn't get that.
They'd wonder what shrines are.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.850
Well, I guess they know that.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.650
"Tsuchi to kaze"...

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.320
What about you, Fukuda?

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:05.740
Well, I was in noise rock bands,

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.750
called Emergency Elevator,
and Emergency Staircase.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.020
Emergency Staircase? Emergency Elevator?

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:12.550
Emergency Elevator was the band name?
Emergency Staircase? Emergency Elevator?

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.140
Yeah, I formed the band.
Emergency Elevator was the band name?
Emergency Staircase? Emergency Elevator?

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.790
Yeah, I formed the band.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.540
You did?

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.120
It was a noise rock band.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:20.320
That's too funny.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.380
But why?
That's too funny.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.090
But why?

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.620
I wasn't expecting Emergency Elevator.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:24.860
Emergency Elevator, the noise rock band.
I wasn't expecting Emergency Elevator.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:25.940
Might be good, though.
Emergency Elevator, the noise rock band.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:26.810
Emergency Elevator, the noise rock band.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:27.880
Hitsujibungaku would be Emergency Elevator.
Emergency Elevator, the noise rock band.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.130
Hitsujibungaku would be Emergency Elevator.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:42.610
Since the band formed in high school,
you've worked together for a long time.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.300
How have you influenced each other
as fellow band members and friends?

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.440
Since we've been together for six years?

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.140
Yeah.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:04:01.540
We're more like brothers and sisters
in arms or siblings than friends.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.260
You're right. We don't really hang out.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.420
Yeah.
You're right. We don't really hang out.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.530
But we're all really different.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:09.490
Yeah.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:10.410
If we were in the same class,
we'd sit in different parts of the room.
We're opposites.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:20.690
If we were in the same class,
we'd sit in different parts of the room.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.170
We might not even end up in the same class.

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.770
Yeah, maybe not.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.470
But we do learn from each other.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:37.330
Like Fukuda always says,
"You should do what you want."

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.090
I think that's true.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.140
Yeah.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.550
And Yurika says,
"I'm not going to try hard."

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:54.570
It makes me think, "Okay, sure,"
but we do learn a lot from each other.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:01.230
I used to be the kind of person
who never wanted to do things

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.500
that might reduce the quality of my work,

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:15.690
but Shiotsuka's way of thinking
and sensibility are impressive.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:26.040
Shiotsuka feels things
much more deeply than I do.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.280
I've really been influenced
by that. I respect it.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.110
Thank you. You're making me
blush, but thank you very much.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.140
Next question, please.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.850
Congratulations on the release of your

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:49.210
Jujutsu Kaisen Shibuya Incident Arc
tie-up song "more than words."

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:49.820
Thank you.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:50.620
Thank you.
Thank you.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:58.330
With its incredible harmonies
and substance, it's a song full of joy.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:03.490
What was the process
for writing this song like?

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:11.490
We wrote four different songs
and got feedback from friends.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:16.730
This was the most trouble
we've ever had writing a song.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:26.240
But as a result, we created something
without compromising on anything.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:35.450
It's the first time I felt like
I really worked hard on a song.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:49.630
Were any of you fans of the JJK manga or
anime before you were asked to write a song?

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.590
If so, do you have
a favorite character or scene?

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:55.910
Yurika, you said you read the manga, right?

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.370
Yeah, and also watched the anime.
Yurika, you said you read the manga, right?

00:06:58.370 --> 00:06:59.290
Yeah, and also watched the anime.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.300
What's your favorite character or scene?

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:09.650
My favorite character is Maki Zenin.
Do you know who that is?

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:14.240
She has glasses and a ponytail.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:16.960
Oh, her?

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.110
I really like her.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.020
Why? I think I get it, though.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:32.960
She's strong-willed and hard-working.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.820
She works just as hard as any of the boys.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.900
She can't use cursed energy—they used
cursed energy instead of "magic"—

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:52.840
but she compensates for it
with her physical abilities.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.200
She's a tsundere.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:04.590
She works really hard, but she's actually
worried about her little sister and family.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:08.760
I like that gap.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:09.490
It's gap moe, then?

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.340
It really is. I really like it.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.730
Do you have a favorite scene?

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.780
Do you?

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:26.200
I bought all the JJK manga volumes
when we received the offer.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:33.280
The Grasshopper Curse made me consider
issues of coexistence with humans,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:40.450
environmental issues,
and how only humans survived.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:52.180
The themes were more thought-provoking than
that of other media I've consumed until now.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.920
That's in the Shibuya Incident Arc.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:54.570
Yeah.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.890
Okay, thank you.

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:01.650
You've produced music
for other anime including

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:04.600
&lt;i&gt;The Heike Story&lt;/i&gt; and
&lt;i&gt;The House of the Lost on the Cape&lt;/i&gt;.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.460
Did you ever expect your band
would create songs for anime?

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:15.200
Would you like to continue
making music for anime?

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.070
What do you think?

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:24.060
I didn't expect it at first.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:25.590
Really?

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:35.910
If anything, I thought we'd be picked up
for live-action or movies instead.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:46.090
Anime has a lot of cool songs
that get the adrenaline pumping.

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:51.520
Hitsujibungaku produces a lot of
low- or mid-tempo songs.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:56.220
It's not something
I imagined we'd be doing.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:58.820
What about you?

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:04.570
Anime tie-up songs tend to prefer
a mainstream rather than underground sound,

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:19.580
J-pop over alternative, which is something
I keep in mind when working on tie-up songs.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:30.060
For the JJK song, I used
a four-on-the-floor beat for the drums,

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:36.620
which resulted in
a new sound for Hitsujibungaku.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:38.610
It was a valuable experience.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.810
For &lt;i&gt;The Heike Story&lt;/i&gt; and
&lt;i&gt;The House of the Lost on the Cape&lt;/i&gt;,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.610
we performed the opening themes.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:52.180
It was like creating a musical
version of those series.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:11:00.300
We were asked to create
&lt;i&gt;The Heike Story&lt;/i&gt; in song form.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:08.590
That was a lot of fun, but this time,
we were asked to create an ending theme.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:16.010
We were establishing our own world

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:21.980
while creating something
that matched the feel of the series.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:31.260
I enjoyed creating something
that matched the feel of the series

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:38.250
while also including ourselves
and our own interests.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:49.640
We got to do things we wouldn't normally
get to do or don't have the time to do.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:56.480
In that regard,
writing songs for anime is interesting,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.550
so I'd like to do it again
if we have the opportunity.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:14.810
During the pandemic, your
interactions with international fans

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:20.250
have included the release of your
album and online concert &lt;i&gt;Hidden Place.&lt;/i&gt;

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:28.330
What would it mean to you to
perform in front of a live audience?

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.590
Is there anywhere you'd like to perform?

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:36.330
&lt;i&gt;Hidden Place.&lt;/i&gt;

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:40.200
We didn't get to perform
because of COVID-19,

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:47.900
but performing in front a live audience
is what concerts are really all about.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:59.810
People often say how audiences
differ depending on the location

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:08.630
and how performances
change according to the audience.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:16.940
That's unique to live performances.

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:22.310
Our audiences tend to be really quiet.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:39.460
Even before COVID-19, we never really asked
our audiences to make noise or hype them up.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:55.520
It felt like our audiences were quietly
and intently listening to us perform.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.270
Yeah, you're right.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:09.880
But even if the pandemic hadn't happened,
we're older now. Our music has changed.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:14.740
Maybe it's because we were frustrated
we couldn't perform due to the pandemic.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:22.650
But once it ended,
I realized how powerful audiences are.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:30.220
They make noise. We experienced them
making noise because they wanted to,

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:35.530
not because we asked them to.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:46.370
These days I feel so powerful
and energized when we perform.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:52.620
Is there anywhere you'd like to perform?

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:58.100
I've been influenced by
a lot of English bands, so London.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:01.570
We went to London recently.
It was a lot of fun.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.080
I just really want to go.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:09.880
I've never been to the US,
so I'd like to go.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.070
I'd like to go to the US, too.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:17.060
We performed in Canada a long time ago,

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:22.620
but Erika wasn't in the band yet,
so it'd be nice to go together.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.260
There are a lot of places
we haven't been yet.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.810
I'd like to perform overseas.

